{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I understand you've been having abdominal pain. Can you tell me more about it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it's been really bad for about a week. It's all over my belly, not just one spot. I feel bloated and crampy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Any nausea or vomiting?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little nausea, and I threw up once—clear fluid—after I got here. No fever at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:29:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. I also see your gastrostomy tube was changed five days ago because of leakage. Has the pain gotten worse since then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, it’s been getting worse. I’m having loose stools a few times a day, but no blood."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Thanks. We’re going to keep you NPO for now—nothing by mouth—so your stomach can rest. We’ll give fluids through your IV and continue your tube feedings slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. Should I stop my regular meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, we’ll keep giving your usual medications through the tube, but we’ll crush the pills first. Some of your meds are important for your mood and stomach protection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. I’ve been using Tylenol for pain, but it’s not helping much."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll give you something stronger for now. But first, we need to rule out serious causes. You’ve already had an X-ray, and it didn’t show a blockage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:36:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. What did it show?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:37:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just some normal gas patterns—nothing concerning like a bowel obstruction. But we’re going to do a CT scan tomorrow with contrast to get a better look."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:38:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will that be safe with my bypass surgery history?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2174-05-23 18:39:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’s safe. We do these often in patients with gastric bypass. It will help us check for internal hernias or other issues."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2174-05-24 11:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. Your CT scan is back. Good news—it shows no blockage, no hernia, and your gastrostomy tube is in the right place."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2174-05-24 11:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. So what’s causing the pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2174-05-24 11:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We don’t see a clear structural cause. Given your history, it could be related to gut motility, irritation from the tube, or bacterial overgrowth. Your labs are stable—no infection or anemia."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2174-05-24 11:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had bacterial overgrowth before. Could that be it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2174-05-24 11:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s possible. You’re already on rifaximin, which helps with that. We’ll keep you on it and see if symptoms improve."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2174-05-24 11:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the pain? What about that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2174-05-24 11:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll start you on oral pain meds—oxycodone liquid—and see how you do. Once you’re tolerating that, we can advance your diet slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2174-05-24 11:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I stay on the IV fluids?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2174-05-24 11:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll taper them as you tolerate tube feedings and oral intake. Right now, you’re on a stage 3 diet—blended foods—through the tube."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2174-05-24 14:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just checking in. How’s the pain now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2174-05-24 14:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better, actually. The new pain med helped. I haven’t needed much nausea medicine."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2174-05-24 14:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. We’ll keep monitoring. Try to walk a little each day—it helps your gut move and prevents clots."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2174-05-24 14:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been getting up. Is the heparin shot for blood clots?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2174-05-24 14:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We give it to all patients after surgery or with limited mobility. You’re also wearing compression boots."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your pain is still improving, and you’re tolerating feedings well. We’re thinking you might go home tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. What will I need to do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll stay on the stage 3 diet for now. No straws or gum—can increase gas. Keep crushing your pills and taking your meds, including the new sucralfate for stomach protection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Sucralfate? What does that do?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It coats your stomach and any ulcers to help them heal. You’ll take it four times a day on an empty stomach."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the pain medicine?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Oxycodone liquid as needed, every six hours. But avoid NSAIDs like ibuprofen—they can cause ulcers in your bypass anatomy."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember—Tylenol is safer. What about activity?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can walk and do light activity, but no heavy lifting—nothing over 10 pounds—for six weeks. No abdominal exercises yet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my tube site? It was leaking before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2174-05-25 09:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep it clean and dry. Use the mupirocin ointment twice a day to prevent infection. Showering is okay, but no baths or swimming."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2174-05-26 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing really well. Vital signs are stable, pain is controlled, and you’re eating and moving fine. We’re clearing you to go home today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2174-05-26 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. When should I follow up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2174-05-26 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "With your surgeon in one to two weeks. Call sooner if you develop fever, severe pain, vomiting, or trouble with the tube."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2174-05-26 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2174-05-26 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and we’ll see you soon if needed."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2174-05-23 18:21:00",
    "admission_end": "2174-05-26 10:35:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted for severe diffuse abdominal pain and nausea following a recent gastrostomy tube change, with a history of Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and prior anastomotic ulcer. She was initially kept NPO, then advanced to a stage 3 diet as symptoms improved. A CT scan showed no obstruction, internal hernia, or tube misplacement. Pain was well controlled with oxycodone, and she tolerated tube feedings. She remained afebrile and hemodynamically stable throughout. At discharge, she was ambulatory, pain-controlled, and able to manage oral and tube medications. She was discharged home with instructions to continue sucralfate, avoid NSAIDs, and follow up with surgery. No complications occurred during the stay.",
    "problems": [
      "Unspecified abdominal pain",
      "Nausea with vomiting, unspecified",
      "Encounter for attention to gastrostomy",
      "Bariatric surgery status"
    ]
  }
}
